{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>The one camp, and strike it.</b> מַחֲנֶה serves both as masculine and as feminine: ״אִם־תַּחֲנֶה עָלַי מַחֲנֶה״ (Psalms 27) — feminine; ״הַמַּחֲנֶה הַזֶּה״ (Genesis 33) — masculine. And so there are other words that serve both as masculine and as feminine: ״הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ יָצָא עַל־הָאָרֶץ״ (Genesis 19:23), ״מִקְצֵה הַשָּׁמַיִם מוֹצָאוֹ״ (Psalms 19) — masculine; ״הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ זָרְחָה עַל־הַמַּיִם״ (II Kings 3) — feminine. So too רוּחַ: ״וְהִנֵּה רוּחַ גְּדוֹלָה בָּאָה״ — feminine; ״וַיִּגַּע בְּאַרְבַּע פִּנּוֹת הַבַּיִת״ (Job 1) — masculine; ״וְרוּחַ גְּדוֹלָה וְחָזָק מְפָרֵק הָרִים״ (I Kings 19) — masculine and feminine. So too אֵשׁ: ״וְאֵשׁ יָצְאָה מֵאֵת ה׳״ (Numbers 16) — feminine; ״אֵשׁ לוֹהֵט״ (Psalms 104) — masculine." "<b>And the camp that is left shall escape.</b> Against his will — for I will fight him. He prepared himself for three things: for a gift, for prayer, and for war. For a gift — ״וַתַּעֲבֹר הַמִּנְחָה עַל פָּנָיו״; for prayer — ״אֱלֹהֵי אָבִי אַבְרָהָם״; for war — ״וְהָיָה הַמַּחֲנֶה הַנִּשְׁאָר לִפְלֵיטָה״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "Rashi's final note on רוּחַ in I Kings 19 flags that in that verse the word carries both genders: וְרוּחַ גְּדוֹלָה is feminine while חָזָק is masculine."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:01:48.531374619Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}